Base improved to 6.6, debug 13.4. "no grain" 8.8 vs base 6.6 — still 2.2fps cost from grain normal blend (full-screen semi-transparent image). Hmm, any full-screen overlay costs. The grain is subtle; could remove it during gameplay or lower its size? Actually the grain background is a data URL noise image tiled over 1560x1020, drawn each frame because the canvas behind changes. In software that's ~2fps. Not huge. Keep for aesthetics? The game is playable enough on real GPU. But the FPS is still low in software. Maybe we can auto-detect software rendering and disable grain/blur effects? Chrome exposes `WEBGL_debug_renderer_info`; we could detect SwiftShader via a WebGL context and set a "low effects" flag, disabling grain (and maybe reduce blur scale). This would make the test environment faster and real low-end devices better. That's a nice adaptive feature. But the requirement is outside cone blurry — can't disable blur entirely. We can reduce blur cost on software by using an even lower scale (0.25) and maybe compositing without clip? The clip is cheap.

